# [DAWN](../wg/dawn.html)

## Summary

The Discovery of Agents With Names (DAWN) Working Group held its initial session at IETF 127. The primary goal of the session was orientation rather than decision-making—building a shared map of existing Internet-Drafts against the four core charter deliverables (Terminology, Use Cases, Architecture, and Protocols), identifying overlaps for potential consolidation, and formulating open technical questions.

Responsible Area Director Éric Vyncke and the Working Group Chairs (Frank Brockners and Gautam) emphasized the narrow, targeted scope of the DAWN charter: enabling clients to discover public properties of named AI resources to facilitate subsequent selection and invocation. Advanced catalog management, full-text search, and post-discovery interactions are explicitly out of scope.

The session featured twelve presentations spanning definitions, use case requirements, architectural frameworks, and a wide array of protocol proposals. Key architectural debates centered on the boundary between DNS-based discovery and post-discovery HTTPS/`/.well-known` resolution, security/trust models (WebPKI vs. DNSSEC), DNS message payload limits, and whether protocols like MCP and A2A require a unified abstraction layer. To drive convergence across competing proposals, the working group agreed to plan interim meetings in early 2027.

